Charging Circuit Notes:
An alternative coil WR151580-48F2-G (“large” coil) was identified to replace the existing WR111180-49F5-G (“small” coil) if its power output was found to be lacking.

Testing of the wireless circuit through various configurations (high+low drive on tx vs. high drive only, series vs. parallel resonant tx coil, and the various combinations of small and large coils, flyback vs. resonant on both tx and rx) showed that the best combination was parallel resonant tx with large coil, high drive only, feeding a small coil on rx. This gave a voltage of 3.592V feeding a 470R load.

During testing, the small coil broke - so proceeded with the large+large coil configuration for now for robustness reasons (3.178V into 470R).

Going back to 1k load gave the full 4.9V needed. Increasing the drive current to the tx coil increased this to a point - but self heating of the coil was a problem. A 25% duty cycle was found to be the best - giving a charge current of ~5.5mA into a dead battery, while requiring 130mA into the charger unit.

Adding a (very dead) battery to the system caused it to draw ~3mA and never boot. This was traced back to the nRF which seems to use a large amount of current on high impedance power supplies, which holds the input voltage at the enable voltage of the nRF (1.58V). Various options were tested; what was found to work was adding bulk capacitance across the battery (when dead, they have a very high impedance due to the protection circuit holding them almost open) plus adding a 470k/100k resistor divider + 1u cap on the enable pin for the regulator - other regulators were investigated at the time of ordering the circuit but we went with the currently selected unit because of cost.

Circuit is now working and charging at 5mA - we can increase to 10mA but then current for the tx coil jumps from 130mA to 200mA - we just need to test for heat. At 5mA the unit will recharge the battery from dead in around 20h - but we’re only expecting people to charge from 80% to 100% or so.

The “coil detection” on the driver PCBA has also been tested and limits implemented - the detection is slight but is there, and it’ll give a small bump in voltage when the two coils are co-located and the mutual resonance causes the sense voltage to increase. LED colour changes from “yellow” (looks like it’s green) to white - we can change this to red to blue or something if we prefer.

Charger build notes:
Flashed all four units. One unit had glue in the holes so it was a bit difficult to flash.

Current consumption is lower than I remembered at "25%" duty cycle. This could just be the calibration, or it could be a difference in coils. Testing on a single orthotic shows that the unit is still charging the battery (about a 10mV jump in voltage from 3.825 to 3.834, and leaving on the bench for 5 minutes took it up to 3.839). Charging to be assessed based on charger performance - better to start small.

Testing coil detection - two units functioned as expected, one unit too low, one unit too high. Likely that the coil detection code will have to be moved from PWM off to a single excitation pulse. Coil detection turned off to prevent an issue with the coil not charging an orthotic.

S magnet faces out

Housing hole for LED plugged with (clear) hot glue. PCBA put into housing and glue squirted through the holes.

Coil mounted with VHB tape 0.6mm thick. Wires pushed down. Magnet fitted with S pole facing out. Fit tested against orthotic.

Coil "potted" with black hot glue, keeping the glue across the magnet thin. Hot air used to smooth out the hot glue, then put in front of the fan to cool. Extra hot glue added to fill areas e.g. around the magnet where the glue had sunk after cooling.

Orthotic build notes:
Had to rework one of the units because of a dry joint on the MCU

Added the required rework mods: 2.7M/1M resistor divider + 1u on the enable of the regulator; 100u cap on the battery terminals (soldered directly to the terminals).

Wrapped kapton tape around each set of 10 pins to prevent them shorting.

Flashed and tested with the generic firmware. Found an error in the firmware - the pin mapping for LHS had not been updated for the new PCBs (so the output ON signal was not connected). Fixed this and all FSR/CAP channels seemed to work.

Using the debugger to power the units - noticed that temperature sensors did not work. Assuming this is because of the low voltage (as battery gets low, the temp sensor output voltage drops so the unit can't detect the change anymore)

Added tape to the bottom of the battery (VHB 1.1mm) but didn't affix to PCB - leave for Reid if changes are needed.

Batteries all measured good at 3.8V - but have seen some fail (not sure if this is a shipping mode or similar that gets triggered).

Soldered batteries and coils to the boards - batteries needed tinning, wires attached to the side of the capacitor facing in towards the middle of the board. Black hot glue added as strain relief. Coils soldered with wires exiting the base of the PCBA. No strain relief added - magnets fitted with N facing out.
